<!--Post: 12247--><!--Time: 1111804864-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12247"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 6:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi embersage; <BR> I am waiting to be added to the list at catch BTK.com. While <BR>waiting , I thought you might be interested in this. I posted it to the <BR>Kansas.com site about a week ago. I can&#39;t say whether or not BTK suspect Dennis Rader is guilty or not, that <BR>is up to the courts and a  jury to decide. And during the trial we will get <BR>more information to help us decide in our own minds for sure. <BR>However, I can share a rather odd incident that happened with Dennis Rader <BR>in my presence in April of 99. <BR>I live about an hour from Park City and have rented a nice building in the <BR>park on North Hydraulic a few times over the years on behaf of a regional <BR>dog club I belonged to. Whenever our breed had a supported entry at the <BR>Sunflower Cluster of dogs shows held at the Kansas Colesium we liked to <BR>have our meeting away from the show site. The building at Park City was <BR>perfect for this as you turned south on Hydraulic from Gate 3 and went 2 <BR>miles to the park. A pizza hut was another half mile to mile on the same road. <BR>Mr Rader would stop by at some point when we used the building &#40;in <BR>uniform&#41; and check to see that we were following the cities request that we <BR>keep all the dogs on lead, even in the building. I think one time he stayed <BR>and helped us lock up. Also anytime anyone stopped by to use that park with <BR>their dogs during the dog show weekend he would check up. He told us that <BR>he patrolled heavy on dog show weekend. <BR>The last time we used the building was in 99. 4 or5 people had stayed <BR>after the meeting to help my husband and I clean up . My husband was <BR>vaccuming in the main room with his back to the door &#40;we always vaccumed to <BR>make sure we left no mud or hair from the dogs&#41; A couple of people were <BR>going over some items for the National Speciality the next year &#40;with their <BR>backs to the door&#41; The others were in the kitchen cleaning utensils and <BR>taking out the trash. I came back to the main room from checking the <BR>bathroom area. Mr. Rader had come into the building apparently unoticed by <BR>the others, except my bitch who gave her short someone not of the group is <BR>here bark that I reconized from hearing her do it at training classes when <BR>someone was way late coming into class-was what brought me back to the main <BR>room. Mr. Rader was by the opened door. He would glance at my husband then <BR>toward the kitchen area, look over his shoulder at the opened inner door. <BR>Then take a slow  sliding step toward the door. I saw him do this a couple <BR>of times before he noticed me. I went over to greet him as I always did. <BR>When I got almost to him he quickly stepped to the side of the door, grabed <BR>the door and put part of his body behind it. Then told me he was checking <BR>that the broken lock had been fixed.  <BR>When he came back from sort of behind the door he had his right hand <BR>behind his back. And he had looked a little rattled from the time he <BR>noticed me. I was already thinking that the body language and what he said <BR>he was doing didn&#39;t match up. I had my male dog with me and told him to go <BR>visit. Something I do anytime I have a chance to work my dog with someone <BR>in uniform. This dog I could give the command to go visit and he would step <BR>up to whoever I pointed to and look up, or he would go lean into their leg. <BR>This time he didn&#39;t want anything to do with Mr Rader- and he had let Mr <BR>Rader pet him on other occasions. So as I was leaning over my dog and <BR>started to hand Mr. Rader a cookie to give him. I was kind of looking up <BR>towards Mr Rader when I noticed He had turned his right side slightly away <BR>from me ,that his right hand was slowly working its way toward his front <BR>pocket. He had a plastic baggie in his hand. I couldn&#39;t see what was in the <BR>bag as he had four fingers palm down over the bag. But I got the impression <BR>whatever it was was&#40;flat, thin&#41; possibly large enough he couldn&#39;t close his <BR>fist over it. And wide enough that he had trouble getting it into his front <BR>pocket when his hand reached the pocket. I couldn&#39;t see the thumb side of <BR>the bag because he had it  very close to his body. The whole  body language <BR>was to me like a kid or teenage caught with something he is trying to hide <BR>from his parents and stealthily sliding toward his pocket. <BR>I straighted up as he got it in his pocket and handed him the cookie. <BR>Glancing at his name tag as his attention was focused on my dog. I know it <BR>was Mr Rader because I set the name in my mind for later by using Radar <BR>from Mash. When he held the cookie toward my male , he still wouldn&#39;t have <BR>anything to do with Mr. Rader. Averted his head and backed away. I laughed <BR>and said he must know you are the dog catcher. Mr Rader said something <BR>about just finishing transporting a wild animal of some kind&#40;don&#39;t remember <BR>what kind now&#41; in the live trap in his truck. I didn&#39;t think too much more <BR>about it because my male didn&#39;t like odd smells on peoples hands. <BR>Mr Rader visited for a few more minutes then left. As soon as he was gone <BR>I asked my husband  &#40;who had come up with his barking dog in time to see my <BR>male not want anything to do with him&#41; if he had seen the whole thing. He <BR>hadn&#39;t so I told him about it and told him I wanted to check out behind the <BR>door for loose paneling or some other spot to hide something. My thinking <BR>at the time is that it may have been drugs or dirty photos that he was <BR>trying to stash out of sight for later. If we had found someplace to hide <BR>something I would have said something to the policeman on duty when we <BR>returned the building key. My husband had a good laugh and teased me a lot <BR>on the way home. Since we saw who they arrested as the BTK suspect it <BR>hasn&#39;t been so funny. We still have no real idea what was in the baggie. <BR>But can maybe guess &#40; I read a lot of true crime stuff have since my mom <BR>got me started in my teens&#41; could have been one of his crime trophies he <BR>wanted to savor in a more stimulating &#39;risky&#39; area&#40;if he is indeed BTK&#41;. Or <BR>he may have been trying to hide himself behind the door. He may have <BR>noticed on other occasions that my husband and I are the last two to leave <BR>as we always went on a walk through to double check everything on the check <BR>list was done , before locking up and returning the key. I know it really <BR>bothered me at the time wondering what an officer in uniform felt he had to <BR>hide from me- it just didn&#39;t make sense. It&#39;s also possible&#40;less likely&#41; <BR>that he stole one of the round leather medallions with our breeds dog head <BR>hand stamped and painted on the front. I had done up several prototypes to <BR>give to the National Speciality liaison to take to the next National <BR>meeting for the committee to make a final choice on head design. &#40;as far as <BR>I know none went missing though- as they were returned to me later that <BR>spring&#41; <BR>Mr Rader had up to this encounter always been very friendly and helpful, <BR>though slightly reserved in a manner I have seen many police officers be <BR>arround people they don&#39;t know. Old school would call it professional <BR>distance. I never even thought about the fact that the compliance officer <BR>wasn&#39;t a regular police officer, even though he also wears a simular <BR>uniform to the police dept. This particular year though one of our out of <BR>town guests had been to the park the day before the meeting and  should <BR>have known better had her dogs off leash and had a little run in with Mr . <BR>Rader. I found out about this when I was explaining the leash rules even in <BR>the building, to the group as people arrived for the meeting. <BR>When I didn&#39;t see in the paper or on tv that they had searched that <BR>building, I did call the hot line and feeling a little silly told them <BR>about the odd incident.  <BR>I would also like to see the police put up a website and in newpaper and <BR>on tv photos of the personal items they found and haven&#39;t identified as to <BR>who they belonged to. Who knows what any one of us might be able to tell <BR>them who something belonged to. I know it will bother me for a long time <BR>that I will probably never know what was in that bag. Probably the only <BR>hope I would have is if author Robert Beattie gets a chance to interview Mr <BR>Rader after the trial is all over and asked him. Not likely . Who knows if <BR>Mr Rader would even remember the incident , or tell the truth if he did. <BR>Heck, I might not even noticed what was going on if I hadn&#39;t learned a <BR>long time ago in my job to watch for behavior that doesn&#39;t fit. I work for <BR>a vending company delivering pop and candy to locations for about l8 years. <BR>Back in the 80&#39;s&#40;?&#41; when a woman who put up L&#39;eggs panty hose displays in <BR>grocery stores in the Salina area disappeared and turned up murdered later <BR>- every man at every location I had on route expressed concern for my <BR>safety. Everyone knew where I would be and when. Wasn&#39;t too much later that <BR>a klu klux clan murder took place in the town I serviced too. I learned <BR>then to plan my route to have the safest time frame to be at a location. To <BR>always be watching as I work. Because when filling machines you have your <BR>back to others in the room to check behind me for others in the area as I <BR>fill. At certain locations to use my feet and elbows to keep working space <BR>if someone got into my space and got me hemed in between open door and <BR>machine, and make it look like I had no idea they were there and it was an <BR>accident that I hit them. To be willing to skip a location and come back <BR>later if I didn&#39;t like the looks of people hanging around at more public <BR>stops. <BR>Also to be aware of what is going on around me when I take the dogs out to <BR>train at parks or just walking down the street. I keep a can of pepper <BR>spray on the back of my front door should I open the door and someone try <BR>to push their way in. I have my hand on it if I don&#39;t reconize the person <BR>on the other side of the door. Of course these days with large l00 lb dogs <BR>barking on the inside only a stupid person would try it <IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> My mother always <BR>said you can&#39;t be too careful when you are a woman out alone. <BR>I&#39;ve been reading this forum off and on for a while, didn&#39;t have anthing <BR>to say until I found out who the BTK suspect arrested is. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12247-->
<!--Post: 12258--><!--Time: 1111809317-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12258"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 7:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, both Ember and the author!  Hope you get on the board soon.  Very interesting.  Actually, these little snippets of Rader&#39;s life are probably far more revealing than thousands of hours of church meetings and picnics, where he was presumably in full &#34;good Dennis&#34; mode.  Whatever it was in the baggie, I&#39;m sure he was up to no good.  My first thought was the old chloroform rag, but I wouldn&#39;t be surprised if it was something he might use to tempt a dog into disobedience, or into an incident to which he could respond and control.  Just like a firebug firefighter.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12258-->
<!--Post: 12261--><!--Time: 1111810302-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12261"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 8:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wow, Was it the Park City Community Building you were at? The Pizza Hut is just a half mile south from there. Down the same street is the Park City Pool and McClean Ball Fields. Huh, I would sure love to know what was in that bag. Do you remember when that was you encountered this behavior from DTDC?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12261-->
<!--Post: 12269--><!--Time: 1111815138-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12269"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 9:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It could have been something to drug a dog or dogs with to make him sleep or die so he could have his &#34;fun&#34; with the people.  Or it could have been a knife or a very small handgun, I suppose.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12269-->
<!--Post: 12274--><!--Time: 1111823871-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12274"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 11:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am sure the author and spouse, think about this all the time now, I wrote and thanked them and ask them if they could answer your questions. I believe we will understand more, by talking to the people Like Rocky and others that actually had to live thru this horror<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12274-->
<!--Post: 12281--><!--Time: 1111834537-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12281"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 26, 2005 - 2:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It sounds to me like he was going to &#34;pass&#34; something. Who were the other four or five people there?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12281-->
<!--Post: 12288--><!--Time: 1111882030-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12288"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 26, 2005 - 4:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky; Yes I believe that is what it is called. It&#39;s on North Hydraulic at <BR>the ?Lyons? park. I have reserved the building again this year for my club <BR>to use. <BR> <BR>Foxfire; I really don&#39;t think it was drugs .Even though one of my first <BR>thoughts back at that time was &#39;cop lifted drugs from someone and was <BR>stashing for his own use later&#39;. But even back then my underlying <BR>impression always turned back to photos. Because even though I couldn&#39;t <BR>&#39;see&#39; what was in the bag I really got the impression that it was square or <BR>rectangle in shape and took up 1/2 to 3/4 of the bag. And when he tried to <BR>put it in his pocket it got caught on the edges of his pocket and was semi <BR>stiff. I&#39;ll say at the end of the post with what I know now about BTK what <BR>my theory is <BR> <BR>Embersage; I think about it a lot. My husband still thinks I am silly about <BR>it. He was startled at first. It did take us a few days after hearing about <BR>Mr Rader being arrested to remember everything. My first reaction was shock <BR>and a little sick to my stomach. Then a few days of &#39;oh my God , what if&#39;. <BR>I remembered from the first that it was Rader and there was something <BR>strange about him and the door. Some semi sleepless nights. For several <BR>days at work something kept niggling at the back of my mind. Hubby wasn&#39;t <BR>real sure about remembering it was Rader at first, until we started talking <BR>about Harvey&#39;s reaction to him that is what jogged my memory about the <BR>whole thing with the sand bag.Then hubby got a little shaky for about a <BR>day. Now he&#39;s back to oh well nothing happened.  <BR> <BR>Bmodule; Pass something to someone in our dog club LOL. Sorry , I don&#39;t buy <BR>that at all. It would be more likely that he snatched something like one of <BR>my medallions off of the ways and means table. But don&#39;t think so because <BR>of the shape of the bag. What was in the bag gave me the impression of <BR>being much bigger than my medallions <BR> <BR>Okay my theories of possible things that might have been in the bag. <BR>Drivers liscense and/or trophy photos from one of the murders. From <BR>reading true crime stories about serial killers &#39;fondling&#39; such trophies in <BR>the presense of others &#40;though out of sight or off to the side&#41; wouldn&#39;t be <BR>&#39;out of the norm&#39;.  <BR>If he was truely trying to slide behind the door unoticed he may have been <BR>wanting to jerk off with his trophy behind the door, a naughty secret <BR>thrill. Or he may have wanted to hide and wait for us to close up. Or just <BR>practicing his skills at sneaking around.But with a good &#39;reason&#39; for being <BR>there if caught by someone. The other time that he was around at the end <BR>and I think I told him one time when we met him earlier in the day at the <BR>park, That we were the ones that got the key and were responsible for <BR>locking up. It was our routine to put the dogs in the van and go back in <BR>and double check the check list before locking up. We are the last two to <BR>leave. <BR>Yes there were two vehicles in the lot. But last time the last two <BR>vehicles in the lot were ours. This year 99 we had one van and we had two <BR>people car pool with us. The other people had car pooled from the show <BR>site. He was probably surpised at how many were in the building when he <BR>came in. <BR>My last theory is this and I just thought about it yesterday when reading <BR>some of the threads about what was in some of the packages that BTK left <BR>for someone to find. <BR>Okay April l0 weekend in 99 would have been a few days past the 25th <BR>anniversary of April 4,l974 death of Kathryn Bright. Does it strike anyone <BR>else as odd that he waited until the 30th anniversary of the murders to <BR>send messages to taunt police? I know the paper ran a 30th anniversary <BR>article on the murders and it took two months after that for BTK to resond. <BR>But doesn&#39;t it make more sense for a serial killer to send something on the <BR>20 or 25th anniversary even though the papers might or might not mention it? <BR>What if what was in that bag was something like the photo copy of <BR>Wegerle&#39;s drivers liscense and 3 pictures taken by the killer. Or the 3x5 <BR>cards with reduced photos and a note for the police chief delivered in a <BR>sand bag? Could he have intended to leave the baggie to be found by either <BR>us, or by whoever cleans the building in the park? If so being caught in <BR>the middle of doing that might have scared him off of that anniversary and <BR>have him waiting. He wouldn&#39;t want to try again that year in case someone <BR>remembered a simular ziploc incident and put it together .Then the 30th <BR>article comes out and he gets nerve enough to try again?  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
